WN.lb(17,{"mt_1VmTUxBrNd":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"numeric","id":"m1","prompt":"A right-angled triangle has shorter sides {=5*k} and {=12*k}. How long is the hypotenuse?","answer":"13*k","hint":"a² + b², then square root.","explain":"{=25*k*k} + {=144*k*k} = {=169*k*k}, √ = {=13*k}.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"The hypotenuse is {=17*k} cm and one side is {=8*k} cm. How long is the other side, in cm?","answer":"15*k","hint":"c² − a².","explain":"{=289*k*k} − {=64*k*k} = {=225*k*k}, √ = {=15*k} cm.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A park is 40 m by 30 m. How much shorter is the diagonal than walking round two edges?","choices":["20 m","10 m","0 m"],"answer":0,"hint":"The diagonal is √(40² + 30²).","explain":"Diagonal = 50 m, edges = 70 m, so 20 m shorter."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam says the hypotenuse of a 3-4 triangle is 7. What went wrong?","choices":["He added the lengths, not the squares","He forgot to double","Nothing, 7 is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Square first.","explain":"9 + 16 = 25 and √25 = 5. Adding the lengths gives 7, which is too long."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A TV screen is {=16*k} inches wide and {=12*k} inches tall. How long is its diagonal, in inches?","answer":"20*k","hint":"Square, add, square root.","explain":"{=256*k*k} + {=144*k*k} = {=400*k*k}, √ = {=20*k}.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"If the angle between sides 3 and 4 is 120°, the third side is…","choices":["longer than 5","exactly 5","shorter than 5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Opening the angle pushes the ends apart.","explain":"A wider angle makes c longer, so c² is bigger than a² + b²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A ramp rises {=7*k} m over a flat distance of {=24*k} m. How long is the ramp, in m?","answer":"25*k","hint":"The ramp is the hypotenuse.","explain":"{=49*k*k} + {=576*k*k} = {=625*k*k}, √ = {=25*k} m.","params":{"k":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"The two shorter sides of a right-angled triangle are {k}×3 cm and {k}×4 cm. How long is the hypotenuse, in cm?","answer":"5*k","hint":"Square both, add, then take the square root.","explain":"({=3*k})² + ({=4*k})² = {=25*k*k}, and √{=25*k*k} = {=5*k} cm.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A ladder {=13*k} m long has its foot {=5*k} m from a wall. How high does it reach, in m?","answer":"12*k","hint":"Subtract the squares for a shorter side.","explain":"({=13*k})² − ({=5*k})² = {=144*k*k}, √{=144*k*k} = {=12*k} m.","params":{"k":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which side is the hypotenuse?","choices":["The side opposite the right angle","The bottom side","The shortest side"],"answer":0,"hint":"It is always the longest side.","explain":"The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle, and it is the longest side."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A triangle has sides 6, 8 and 11. Is it right-angled?","choices":["No: 36 + 64 = 100, not 121","Yes: 6 + 8 > 11","Yes, every triangle is"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check a² + b² against c².","explain":"36 + 64 = 100, but 11² = 121, so it is not right-angled (the angle is wider than 90°)."}],"explain":{"kid":["The longest side of a right-angled triangle is the hypotenuse. It is opposite the right angle.","Build a square on each side. The two small squares together fill the big one exactly: a² + b² = c².","To find the long side, add the squares, then take the square root. To find a short side, subtract the squares, then take the square root."],"adult":["Pythagoras' theorem holds only for right-angled triangles: a² + b² = c², where c is the hypotenuse. If the angle is wider than 90°, c² is bigger than a² + b²; if narrower, smaller.","The classic error is working with lengths instead of areas: 3 + 4 is not 5. Square, add or subtract, then square root. A quick check: the hypotenuse must be the longest side."]},"familyTip":"Measure a door or a TV: its width and height, then work out the diagonal and check it with a tape measure.","keyIdea":"In a right-angled triangle the squares on the two shorter sides add up to the square on the longest side: a² + b² = c².","visual":{"type":"squares","caption":"Square numbers are areas: 3² = 9, 4² = 16, 5² = 25. Pythagoras adds areas like these.","opts":{"n":5}},"lab":{"type":"flagpyth","caption":"Flagship lab: the park shortcut. Drag the squares on the sides into the square on the hypotenuse.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_XJJlJ6IQHx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"The point on the unit circle at 210° is (−√3/2, −1/2). What are sin 210° and cos 210°?","choices":["sin 210° = −1/2 and cos 210° = −√3/2","sin 210° = −√3/2 and cos 210° = −1/2","sin 210° = 1/2 and cos 210° = √3/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"The first coordinate is cos θ and the second is sin θ.","explain":"x = cos 210° = −√3/2 and y = sin 210° = −1/2. Both are negative because 210° is in quadrant III."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Why is cos θ negative for angles in the second quadrant?","choices":["Points there are left of the y-axis, so their x-coordinate is negative","Angles bigger than 90° always have a negative cosine","Sine and cosine swap signs every 90°"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos θ is one of the point's coordinates. Which one, and where is the point?","explain":"cos θ is the x-coordinate, and every point in quadrant II is left of the y-axis. Size alone is not the reason: cos 300° = 1/2 is positive."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An angle of {a}° is in quadrant III. What is its reference angle, in degrees?","answer":"a-180","hint":"In quadrant III, measure past the negative x-axis, at 180°.","explain":"{a}° − 180° = {=a-180}°, so the reference angle is {=a-180}°. Sine and cosine are both negative there.","params":{"a":[181,269]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Which angle between 0° and 360° lands on the same point of the unit circle as {=a+360*k}°?","answer":"a","hint":"Take off full turns of 360° until the angle is less than 360°.","explain":"Subtract {=360*k}°, which is {k} full turns: {=a+360*k}° − {=360*k}° = {a}°.","params":{"a":[10,350],"k":[1,3]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A point on the unit circle is at (0.6, −0.8). Which statement is true?","choices":["cos θ = 0.6 and sin θ = −0.8, so θ is in quadrant IV","cos θ = −0.8 and sin θ = 0.6, so θ is in quadrant II","cos θ = 0.6 and sin θ = −0.8, so θ is in quadrant II"],"answer":0,"hint":"x is cos θ and y is sin θ. Right of the y-axis and below the x-axis is which quadrant?","explain":"x = 0.6 = cos θ and y = −0.8 = sin θ. Right and below is quadrant IV. Check: 0.6² + 0.8² = 0.36 + 0.64 = 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"In a right triangle with hypotenuse 1, which length is the side adjacent to the acute angle θ?","choices":["cos θ","sin θ","tan θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos θ = adjacent ÷ hypotenuse, and the hypotenuse is 1.","explain":"Adjacent ÷ 1 = cos θ, so the adjacent side has length cos θ. That is why the unit circle's x-coordinate is cos θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is sin(−90°)?","choices":["−1","1","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"A negative angle turns clockwise. Where does a quarter turn clockwise from (1, 0) end?","explain":"Clockwise 90° ends at (0, −1), the same point as 270°, so sin(−90°) = −1 and cos(−90°) = 0."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which angle has the same sine as 40°?","choices":["140°","220°","320°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Sine is the height. Which other point on the circle is at the same height as 40°?","explain":"180° − 40° = 140° is the mirror image across the y-axis, at the same height. 220° and 320° are below the x-axis, so their sines are negative."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"A point on the unit circle in quadrant I has cos θ = 0.28. What is sin θ?","choices":["0.96","0.72","0.28"],"answer":0,"hint":"Every point is 1 from the centre, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1.","explain":"sin²θ = 1 − 0.28² = 1 − 0.0784 = 0.9216, so sin θ = 0.96 (positive in quadrant I). 0.72 comes from 1 − 0.28, which forgets the squares."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"On the unit circle, what are the coordinates of the point at angle θ?","choices":["(cos θ, sin θ)","(sin θ, cos θ)","(θ, 1)"],"answer":0,"hint":"The radius is a hypotenuse of length 1. Which side is adjacent to θ: across or up?","explain":"The across distance is adjacent ÷ 1 = cos θ, and the height is opposite ÷ 1 = sin θ. So the point is (cos θ, sin θ)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"An angle of {a}° is in quadrant II. What is its reference angle, in degrees?","answer":"180-a","hint":"In quadrant II, measure the gap between the radius and the negative x-axis, at 180°.","explain":"{a}° is {=180-a}° short of 180°, so the reference angle is {=180-a}°. The point is at the same height as the point at {=180-a}°.","params":{"a":[91,179]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Turning clockwise by {a}° gives the angle −{a}°. Which angle between 0° and 360° lands on the same point?","answer":"360-a","hint":"A full turn is 360°. Going back {a}° from a full turn ends in the same place.","explain":"Clockwise {a}° ends where anticlockwise 360° − {a}° = {=360-a}° does, so both angles have the same sine and cosine.","params":{"a":[1,359]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is true for an angle in quadrant III, between 180° and 270°?","choices":["cos θ < 0 and sin θ < 0","cos θ < 0 and sin θ > 0","cos θ > 0 and sin θ < 0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Quadrant III is bottom left. Is x positive or negative there? And y?","explain":"Bottom left means left of the y-axis (x < 0) and below the x-axis (y < 0), so both cos θ and sin θ are negative."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"What is cos 150°?","choices":["−√3/2","√3/2","−1/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the reference angle, then ask whether the point is left or right of the y-axis.","explain":"The reference angle is 30° and the point is left of the y-axis, so cos 150° = −cos 30° = −√3/2 ≈ −0.866. −1/2 would be cos 120°."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw a circle with radius 1 and its centre at (0, 0). Start a point at (1, 0) and turn it round the circle the opposite way to a clock's hands. Call the angle it turns θ.","Read the point's place. How far across it is, x, is called cos θ. How far up it is, y, is called sin θ.","On the left side of the circle, x is less than zero, so cos θ is too. Below the middle line, y is less than zero, so sin θ is too.","At 150°, the point is up and to the left. So cos 150° is negative and sin 150° is positive."],"adult":["Take the circle x² + y² = 1. Turn a radius anticlockwise from the positive x-axis through θ. The end point is defined to be (cos θ, sin θ). For an acute angle this matches SOH CAH TOA: the radius is a hypotenuse of length 1, so the adjacent side is x and the opposite side is y.","The definition works for any angle: 150°, 210°, negative angles (turned clockwise) and angles over 360°. Angles that differ by a full turn, 360° or 2π radians, land on the same point, so sine and cosine repeat.","The quadrant gives the sign. Cosine is positive in quadrants I and IV, right of the y-axis. Sine is positive in I and II, above the x-axis. The size comes from the reference angle, the acute angle to the x-axis. So cos 150° = −cos 30° = −√3/2 and sin 210° = −sin 30° = −1/2.","Every point is 1 from the centre, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1 for all θ. Common mistakes: swapping the coordinates (cos is x, not y), using the reference angle's signs in every quadrant, turning clockwise for a positive angle, and thinking sine and cosine stop at 90°."]},"familyTip":"Hold a pencil flat on a paper plate, from the centre to the right edge. Turn it anticlockwise to about 150° and ask: is the tip left or right of centre, above or below? That gives the signs of cos 150° and sin 150° before any calculator.","keyIdea":"On the unit circle (radius 1, centre at the origin), the point reached by turning θ anticlockwise from the positive x-axis has coordinates (cos θ, sin θ). This defines sine and cosine for every angle, and the quadrant decides their signs.","visual":{"type":"geolab","caption":"Legs 8 and 6, hypotenuse 10: cos = 0.8, sin = 0.6. Scale the hypotenuse to 1 and the legs are the unit-circle point (0.8, 0.6).","opts":{"pts":[[1,1],[9,1],[9,7]],"grid":10}},"lab":{"type":"flagunit","caption":"Flagship lab: turn a point round the unit circle. Its x is cos θ, its y is sin θ, and the graph draws both as you go.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_FhJ3b20GC4":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Is a diameter a chord?","choices":["Yes: it joins two points on the circle, passing through the centre","No: chords cannot pass through the centre","No: a diameter is an arc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Check the definition of a chord. Does a diameter fit it?","explain":"A chord is any straight line joining two points on the circle. A diameter does that, through the centre, so it is the longest chord."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"One straight cut across a round cake, away from the centre, cuts off a small piece. What is that piece?","choices":["A segment","A sector","A tangent"],"answer":0,"hint":"How many straight edges does the piece have, and are they radii?","explain":"The piece is bounded by one chord (the cut) and an arc, so it is a segment. A sector would need two radii."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The diameter of a circle is {d} cm. How long is its radius, in cm?","answer":"d/2","hint":"A diameter is made of two radii end to end through the centre.","explain":"The radius is half the diameter: {d} ÷ 2 = {=d/2} cm.","params":{"d":[4,30]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"OT is a radius and TP is part of the tangent at T. In triangle OTP, angle TOP is {a}°. What is angle OPT, in degrees?","answer":"90-a","hint":"The radius meets the tangent at 90°, so angle OTP is 90°. Then use the angle sum of a triangle.","explain":"Angle OTP is 90°. The angles add up to 180°, so angle OPT is 180 − 90 − {a} = {=90-a}°.","params":{"a":[20,70]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"The minor arc AB is 140°. Which statement is true?","choices":["The major arc AB is 220°","The major arc AB is 140°","The minor arc is the longer of the two"],"answer":0,"hint":"Minor means less than half. The two arcs make a full turn of 360°.","explain":"360° − 140° = 220°, so the major arc is 220°. The minor arc is always the shorter one."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"What is the difference between a sector and a segment?","choices":["A sector has two radii as edges; a segment is cut off by a chord","A sector is cut off by a chord; a segment has two radii as edges","There is no difference"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one looks like a slice of pizza from the middle?","explain":"A sector is bounded by two radii and an arc. A segment is bounded by a chord and an arc."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A straight line cuts a circle at two points. What is the part of the line inside the circle?","choices":["A chord","A tangent","An arc"],"answer":0,"hint":"Its two ends are on the circle and it is straight.","explain":"The inside part joins two points on the circle with a straight line, so it is a chord. A tangent would meet the circle only once."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"A straight line meets a circle at exactly one point and does not cross it. What is the line?","choices":["A tangent","A chord","A radius"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which line only touches the circle and stays outside it?","explain":"That is a tangent. A chord meets the circle at two points, and a radius starts at the centre."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which of these describes a chord?","choices":["A straight line joining two points on the circle","A curved part of the circle's edge","A line from the centre to the edge"],"answer":0,"hint":"A chord is straight, and both of its ends are on the circle.","explain":"A chord joins two points on the circle with a straight line. The curved part is an arc, and a centre-to-edge line is a radius."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A circle has radius {r} cm. How long is the longest chord you can draw in it, in cm?","answer":"2*r","hint":"The longest chord passes through the centre. What is that chord called?","explain":"The longest chord is the diameter, which is two radii: 2 × {r} = {=2*r} cm.","params":{"r":[2,12]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"Points A and B split a circle into two arcs. The minor arc AB is {a}°. What is the major arc AB, in degrees?","answer":"360-a","hint":"The two arcs together go all the way round the circle, which is 360°.","explain":"The two arcs add up to 360°, so the major arc is 360 − {a} = {=360-a}°.","params":{"a":[20,170]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"A region of a circle is bounded by two radii and an arc. What is it called?","choices":["A sector","A segment","A chord"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of a slice of pizza cut from the middle. Which name goes with two radii?","explain":"Two radii and an arc make a sector. A segment is cut off by a chord, and a chord is a line, not a region."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"How many points does a tangent share with its circle?","choices":["Exactly 1","Exactly 2","None"],"answer":0,"hint":"Picture a ruler resting against a coin. Where do they meet?","explain":"A tangent touches the circle at exactly one point. A line meeting it at 2 points cuts through it, and one meeting it at none misses."}],"explain":{"kid":["Think of a round pizza. The crust all the way round is the circumference. A line from the middle to the crust is a radius.","Draw a straight line from one point on the crust to another. That line is a chord. The longest chord goes through the middle. It is the diameter.","A bit of the curved crust is an arc. Two points split the crust into a short arc and a long arc. The short one is the minor arc. The long one is the major arc.","Cut a slice from the middle, with two straight cuts. That slice is a sector. Make one straight cut across the edge instead. The piece you cut off is a segment. A ruler that just rests against the edge, touching at one spot, is a tangent."],"adult":["Circles have named parts beyond radius and diameter. A chord is a straight line segment whose two ends lie on the circle. A diameter is a chord that passes through the centre, so it is the longest possible chord, twice the radius.","An arc is part of the circumference between two points. Any two points make two arcs: the minor arc, less than half the circle, and the major arc, more than half. Their angles at the centre add up to 360°, so a 100° minor arc goes with a 260° major arc.","A sector is the region between two radii and the arc joining their ends, shaped like a slice of pizza. A segment is the region between a chord and its arc, with no radii as edges. Each comes in a minor and a major version.","A tangent is a straight line that touches the circle at exactly one point and does not cross it. The radius drawn to that point meets the tangent at 90°. A line that crosses the circle at two points is not a tangent; the part of it inside the circle is a chord. Common mistakes: swapping sector and segment; thinking a diameter is not a chord; choosing the major arc by its position on the page instead of its size; and calling a line that cuts through the circle a tangent."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper plate or a tortilla. Make one cut from the edge to the middle and back out to make a slice, and one straight cut across the edge. Ask which piece is the sector and which is the segment, and how they know.","keyIdea":"A chord is a straight line joining two points on a circle; the diameter is the longest chord, through the centre. An arc is part of the curved edge: the minor arc is less than half, the major arc more. A sector is a region bounded by two radii and an arc; a segment is a region cut off by a chord. A tangent touches the circle at exactly one point, at 90° to the radius there.","worked":{"title":"A and B are points on a circle with centre O, and the minor arc AB is 70°. Name the parts and find the major arc.","steps":["The straight line AB joins two points on the circle, so it is a chord. It misses O, so it is not a diameter.","The minor arc AB is the short way round: 70°. The major arc goes the long way: 360° − 70° = 290°.","The region between OA, OB and the minor arc is the minor sector. The region between chord AB and the minor arc is the minor segment.","A tangent at A would touch the circle only at A, at 90° to the radius OA."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each part a line, a region or part of the curved edge? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Straight line","Region (an area)","Curved edge"],"items":[["Chord",0],["Diameter",0],["Tangent",0],["Radius",0],["Sector",1],["Segment",1],["Minor arc",2],["Major arc",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagchord","caption":"Flagship lab: drag a chord until it becomes a diameter, pick the major arc, find the sector and the segment, and slide a line until it is a tangent.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_HXwk3pTM1W":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which expression gives the arc length of a sector with angle θ and radius r?","choices":["θ/360 × 2πr","θ/360 × πr²","θ/100 × 2πr"],"answer":0,"hint":"An arc is part of the edge, so start from the circumference.","explain":"The arc is the fraction θ/360 of the circumference 2πr. πr² gives an area, and a full turn is 360°, not 100."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Find the area of a 72° sector of radius 10 cm, to 1 decimal place.","answer":62.8,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"72/360 is 1/5. Find 1/5 of the full circle's area, π × 10².","explain":"1/5 × π × 100 = 20π, which is about 62.8 cm²."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"The radius of a sector doubles and the angle stays the same. What happens to its area?","choices":["It becomes 4 times as big","It doubles","It stays the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"Area uses r². What is (2r)²?","explain":"(2r)² = 4r², so the area is 4 times as big. The arc only doubles, because it uses r, not r²."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"Write the arc of a {=30*k}° sector of radius {r} cm as a multiple of π. Type the number in front of π.","answer":"k*r/6","hint":"Work out {=30*k}/360 of 2π × {r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × 2 × {r} = {=k*r/6}, so the arc is {=k*r/6}π cm.","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,12]},"where":"(k*r)%6==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ali finds the arc of a 60° sector with r = 6 cm as 60/360 × π × 6² = 18.8 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["He used the area formula; the arc is 60/360 × 2π × 6 ≈ 6.3 cm","He should divide by 100","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is an arc a length or an area?","explain":"An arc is a length, so it is a fraction of 2πr: 1/6 × 37.7 ≈ 6.3 cm. 18.8 cm² is the sector's area."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A sector of radius 6 cm has an arc of 5π cm. What is its angle in degrees?","answer":150,"hint":"Divide the arc by the whole circumference, 12π, then multiply by 360.","explain":"5π ÷ 12π = 5/12, and 5/12 of 360° is 150°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"What is the perimeter of a sector?","choices":["The arc length plus two radii","The arc length only","The arc length plus one radius"],"answer":0,"hint":"Trace round the edge of a pizza slice. How many straight edges are there?","explain":"A sector has one curved edge, the arc, and two straight edges, both radii. Add all three."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Write the area of a {=30*k}° sector of radius {r} cm as a multiple of π. Type the number in front of π.","answer":"k*r*r/12","hint":"Work out {=30*k}/360 of π × {r}².","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × {=r*r} = {=k*r*r/12}, so the area is {=k*r*r/12}π cm².","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,8]},"where":"(k*r*r)%12==0"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A sector has angle {=30*k}° and radius {r} cm. Its arc length is how many π cm? (Type the number in front of π.)","answer":"k*r/6","hint":"Fraction of the circle: {=30*k}/360. Multiply it by 2r = {=2*r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × 2π × {r} = {=k*r/6}π cm. The fraction of the circle times the whole edge 2πr.","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[3,12]},"where":"(k*r)%3==0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"A sector has angle {=30*k}° and radius {r} cm. Its area is how many π cm²? (Type the number in front of π.)","answer":"k*r*r/12","hint":"Fraction of the circle: {=30*k}/360. Multiply it by r² = {=r*r}.","explain":"{=30*k}/360 × π × {r}² = {=k*r*r/12}π cm². The fraction of the circle times the whole area πr².","params":{"k":[1,11],"r":[2,10]},"where":"(k*r*r)%3==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What fraction of the whole circle is a 72° sector?","choices":["1/5","72/100","1/72"],"answer":0,"hint":"A full turn is 360°. Simplify 72/360.","explain":"72/360 = 1/5, because 5 × 72 = 360. Dividing by 100 treats the angle like a percentage."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Find the perimeter of a 90° sector of radius 10 cm, to 1 decimal place.","answer":35.7,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"Perimeter = arc + two radii. The arc is 1/4 of 2π × 10.","explain":"Arc = 1/4 × 2π × 10 = 15.7 cm. Add the two straight edges: 15.7 + 10 + 10 = 35.7 cm."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p5","prompt":"A sector of radius {r} cm has an arc of {m}π cm. What is its angle in degrees?","answer":"180*m/r","hint":"Fraction = arc ÷ 2πr = {m}π ÷ {=2*r}π. Then multiply by 360.","explain":"{m}π ÷ (2π × {r}) × 360 = {m} ÷ {=2*r} × 360 = {=180*m/r}°.","params":{"m":[1,9],"r":[2,12]},"where":"(180*m)%r==0 && 180*m/r<360"}],"explain":{"kid":["A sector is a slice of a circle, like a slice of pizza. Its angle θ is at the centre. A full turn is 360°.","First find the fraction. A 90° slice is 90/360, so a quarter. A 72° slice is 72/360, so one fifth.","The arc is the curved crust. It is that fraction of the whole edge, 2πr. With r = 6 cm a quarter arc is 37.7 ÷ 4 = 9.4 cm.","The area is that fraction of the whole circle, πr². With r = 6 cm a quarter slice is 113.1 ÷ 4 = 28.3 cm²."],"adult":["Arc length = θ/360 × 2πr and sector area = θ/360 × πr². Both are the same fraction of a whole-circle formula; only the whole changes, from a length to an area.","Scaling shows the difference. Doubling θ doubles both. Doubling r doubles the arc but multiplies the area by 4, because r is squared.","Working backwards uses the same fraction. An arc of 15.7 cm on r = 6 cm is 15.7 ÷ 37.7 ≈ 0.417 of the edge, so θ ≈ 0.417 × 360 = 150°. The perimeter of a sector is the arc plus two radii.","Common mistakes: dividing θ by 100 instead of 360; using πr² for an arc or 2πr for an area; using πr as the circumference; forgetting to square r; leaving out the two radii in a perimeter; and rounding the fraction early, which drifts the final answer."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper plate into equal slices: 4, then 6, then 8. Measure the whole rim with string first. Ask how long the crust of one slice must be, then check with the string. What happens to the crust if the plate is twice as wide?","keyIdea":"A sector with angle θ is θ/360 of the circle. Its arc is that fraction of 2πr, and its area is that fraction of πr².","worked":{"title":"Find the arc length and area of a 72° sector of radius 10 cm.","steps":["Fraction of the circle: 72/360 = 1/5.","Arc length: 1/5 × 2π × 10 = 4π ≈ 12.6 cm.","Area: 1/5 × π × 10² = 20π ≈ 62.8 cm².","Check: 5 such sectors make the circle. 5 × 12.6 = 62.8 cm ≈ 2π × 10, and 5 × 62.8 = 314 cm² ≈ π × 10²."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each expression a length or an area? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["A length (cm)","An area (cm²)"],"items":[["θ/360 × 2πr",0],["2πr",0],["arc + 2r",0],["θ/360 × πr²",1],["πr²",1],["¼ × π × 6²",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagarc","caption":"Flagship lab: drag the angle and radius of a sector and watch its arc and area as a lit part of the whole circle laid flat; tile copies to see the fraction.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qm2CQajR6R":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"How many radians make a full turn?","choices":["2π, about 6.28","360","π, about 3.14"],"answer":0,"hint":"The edge is 2πr. How many radii is that?","explain":"The circumference 2πr divided by r is 2π, so a full turn is 2π ≈ 6.28 rad. π rad is only half a turn."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"Convert {k}π/4 radians to degrees.","answer":"45*k","hint":"π/4 rad = 180 ÷ 4 = 45°. Multiply by {k}.","explain":"{k}π/4 × 180/π = {k} × 45 = {=45*k}°.","params":{"k":[3,15]},"where":"k%2==1"},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"An arc of {s} cm makes an angle of {=k/2} rad at the centre. Find the radius in cm.","answer":"2*s/k","hint":"Rearrange s = rθ: divide the arc by the angle in radians to get r.","explain":"r = s ÷ θ = {s} ÷ {=k/2} = {=2*s/k} cm.","params":{"s":[3,15],"k":[1,6]},"where":"(200*s)%k==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Sam uses s = rθ for a 60° arc with r = 5 cm and gets 5 × 60 = 300 cm. What went wrong?","choices":["θ must be in radians: 60° = π/3, so s = 5π/3 ≈ 5.24 cm","He should divide by 60","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"s = rθ only works when θ is in radians.","explain":"60° = π/3 rad, so s = 5 × π/3 ≈ 5.24 cm. Putting degrees into s = rθ gives an arc far longer than the whole circle (31.4 cm)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"Convert 1 radian to degrees, to 1 decimal place.","answer":57.3,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"π rad = 180°, so 1 rad = 180 ÷ π.","explain":"1 rad = 180 ÷ π = 57.2957…, so about 57.3°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Write 240° in radians.","choices":["4/3","4π/3","2π/3"],"answer":1,"hint":"240 ÷ 180 = 4/3. Do not lose the π.","explain":"240/180 = 4/3, so 240° = 4π/3 rad. 4/3 drops the π, and 2π/3 is only 120°."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"Convert 2.5 radians to degrees, to the nearest degree.","answer":143,"tolerance":0.5,"hint":"Multiply by 180/π, about 57.3.","explain":"2.5 × 180/π = 143.24…, so about 143°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Circles of radius 3 cm and 6 cm each have an arc that makes 1.2 rad at the centre. Which is true?","choices":["Both arcs are 1.2 cm long","The arcs are 3.6 cm and 7.2 cm, and the angle is the same","The bigger circle's angle is 2.4 rad"],"answer":1,"hint":"Use s = rθ for each circle. Does the angle change?","explain":"s = 3 × 1.2 = 3.6 cm and s = 6 × 1.2 = 7.2 cm. The arc doubles with the radius, but s ÷ r is 1.2 rad for both."}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"How many degrees is {k}π/12 radians?","answer":"15*k","hint":"π rad = 180°, so π/12 rad = 180 ÷ 12 = 15°. Multiply by {k}.","explain":"{k}π/12 × 180/π = {k} × 15 = {=15*k}°.","params":{"k":[5,23]},"where":"k%2==1 && k%3!=0"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the arc length when r = {r} cm and θ = {=k/2} rad.","answer":"r*k/2","hint":"θ is already in radians, so use s = rθ with no 360.","explain":"s = rθ = {r} × {=k/2} = {=r*k/2} cm.","params":{"r":[2,12],"k":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"What is one radian?","choices":["The angle where the arc is as long as the radius","1/360 of a full turn","The angle where the arc is 1 cm long"],"answer":0,"hint":"A radian compares the arc with the radius of the same circle.","explain":"One radian is the angle at the centre when the arc equals the radius. 1/360 of a turn is one degree, and 1 cm depends on the circle's size."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"An arc of {s} cm lies on a circle of radius {r} cm. Find θ in radians.","answer":"s/r","hint":"θ = s ÷ r: how many radius-lengths fit along the arc?","explain":"θ = s ÷ r = {s} ÷ {r} = {=s/r} rad.","params":{"s":[3,20],"r":[2,10]},"where":"(s*100)%r==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Convert 135° to radians.","choices":["3π/4","3/4","135π"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide by 180°, then multiply by π.","explain":"135 ÷ 180 = 3/4, and each 180° is π rad, so 135° = 3π/4 ≈ 2.36 rad. Writing 3/4 drops the π."}],"explain":{"kid":["Take a piece of string as long as the radius of a circle. Lay it round the edge. The angle it makes at the centre is one radian.","One radian is about 57°. It is a big angle, not a tiny one like 1°.","The whole edge is 2πr long, so about 6.28 strings fit round. A full turn is 2π radians. Half a turn is π radians.","With radians, arc length is easy. An angle of 3 radians on a 4 cm circle has an arc of 3 × 4 = 12 cm."],"adult":["An angle in radians is the arc length divided by the radius, θ = s/r. It is a ratio of two lengths, so it has no unit and does not depend on the size of the circle.","The circumference is 2πr, so a full turn is 2πr ÷ r = 2π rad. That gives π rad = 180°. To change degrees to radians multiply by π/180; to change back multiply by 180/π. So 135° = 135/180 × π = 3π/4.","Rearranged, s = rθ. This is the degree formula θ/360 × 2πr with the 360 and the 2π already cancelled, which is why calculus and physics use radians.","Common mistakes: dropping the π (writing 135° as 3/4 rad); using the conversion factor upside down; putting degrees into s = rθ; thinking 1 rad is about 1°; and working out r ÷ s instead of s ÷ r."]},"familyTip":"Cut a string as long as the radius of a plate. Walk it round the rim, marking each length. How many fit? Then ask: would a bigger plate fit more strings, or the same number?","keyIdea":"One radian is the angle at the centre when the arc is as long as the radius, so an angle in radians is arc ÷ radius: θ = s/r. About 6.28 radius-lengths fit round a circle, so a full turn is 2π rad and 180° = π rad. In radians the arc length is simply s = rθ.","worked":{"title":"A circle has radius 8 cm. Find the arc for 135°, then the angle that gives an arc of 20 cm.","steps":["135° = 135/180 × π = 3π/4 rad.","s = rθ = 8 × 3π/4 = 6π ≈ 18.8 cm.","For an arc of 20 cm: θ = s ÷ r = 20 ÷ 8 = 2.5 rad.","In degrees: 2.5 × 180/π ≈ 143.2°. Check: 143.2/360 × 2π × 8 ≈ 20 cm."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which turn is each angle? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Half a turn","A quarter turn","A sixth of a turn"],"items":[["π rad",0],["180°",0],["π/2 rad",1],["90°",1],["π/3 rad",2],["60°",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagrad","caption":"Flagship lab: lay radius-length strings round a circle, see one radian, a full turn of 2π, convert degrees to radians and find arcs with s = rθ.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_0UJ58IxUYT":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A 4 cm side becomes 6 cm on a similar copy. What is the scale factor from the first shape to the copy?","choices":["1.5","2","0.67"],"answer":0,"hint":"Scale factor = new length ÷ old length.","explain":"6 ÷ 4 = 1.5. The difference 6 − 4 = 2 is not a scale factor, and 4 ÷ 6 goes the other way."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = 3 cm, DB = 6 cm and DE = 4 cm. Find BC in cm.","answer":12,"hint":"AB = AD + DB. Compare AB with AD to get k.","explain":"AB = 9 cm, so k = 9 ÷ 3 = 3 and BC = 4 × 3 = 12 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Ali says: '4 cm became 10 cm, so add 6. The 7 cm side becomes 13 cm.' What went wrong?","choices":["He added; similar shapes multiply, so it is 7 × 2.5 = 17.5 cm","He should have taken 6 away","Nothing, it is right"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the copy made by adding to each side or by multiplying each side?","explain":"k = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5, so 7 × 2.5 = 17.5 cm. Adding 6 to every side changes the shape."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AB = {s} cm, BC = {c} cm and DE = {d} cm. Find AD in cm.","answer":"s*d/c","hint":"Set up AD/AB = DE/BC, so AD/{s} = {d}/{c}.","explain":"AD = {s} × {d} ÷ {c} = {=s*d/c} cm. Keep small over big on both sides of the proportion.","params":{"s":[6,15],"c":[4,12],"d":[2,10]},"where":"(s*d)%c==0 && d<c"},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"One triangle has sides 4, 6 and 8 cm. Another has sides 6, 8 and 10 cm. Are they similar?","choices":["No: 6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 but 10 ÷ 8 = 1.25","Yes: each side is 2 cm longer","Yes: they are both triangles"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide each side of the second by the matching side of the first. Are the answers equal?","explain":"The ratios 1.5, 1.33 and 1.25 differ, so there is no single scale factor and the shapes are not similar."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A model car is similar to a real car that is 4 m long and 1.5 m tall. The model is 20 cm long. How tall is the model, in cm?","answer":7.5,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"Use the same units: 4 m = 400 cm. Then k = 20 ÷ 400.","explain":"k = 20 ÷ 400 = 0.05, and 1.5 m = 150 cm, so the model is 150 × 0.05 = 7.5 cm tall."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A similar copy of a triangle has been flipped and turned. How do you find the side that matches the 5 cm side?","choices":["Find the side opposite the same angle","Pick the side in the same place on the page","Pick the longest side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Turning or flipping moves sides around but keeps every angle the same.","explain":"Matching sides sit opposite equal angles. Position on the page changes when a shape is turned, so it cannot be trusted."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"On the bigger of two similar shapes, a {=a*k} cm side matches a {a} cm side. Another side of the bigger shape is {=b*k} cm. How long is the matching side on the smaller shape?","answer":"b","hint":"Going from big to small, divide by k = {=a*k} ÷ {a}.","explain":"k = {k}, so the smaller side is {=b*k} ÷ {k} = {b} cm.","params":{"a":[2,5],"b":[3,8],"k":[2,5]},"where":"a!=b"}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Two triangles are similar. A {a} cm side matches a {=a*k} cm side. How long is the side that matches the {b} cm side?","answer":"b*k","hint":"Find the scale factor first: {=a*k} ÷ {a}. Then multiply {b} by it.","explain":"k = {=a*k} ÷ {a} = {k}, so the matching side is {b} × {k} = {=b*k} cm.","params":{"a":[2,6],"b":[3,9],"k":[2,4]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = {p} cm, DB = {q} cm and DE = {d} cm. How long is BC?","answer":"d*(p+q)/p","hint":"AD matches the whole side AB = {p} + {q}, not DB.","explain":"AB = {=p+q} cm, so k = {=p+q} ÷ {p} and BC = {d} × {=p+q} ÷ {p} = {=d*(p+q)/p} cm.","params":{"p":[2,5],"q":[2,8],"d":[2,6]},"where":"(d*(p+q))%p==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"A 6 cm side matches a 9 cm side on a similar shape. How long is the side that matches 8 cm?","choices":["12 cm","11 cm","5.3 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find k = 9 ÷ 6, then multiply 8 by it.","explain":"k = 1.5, so 8 × 1.5 = 12 cm. 11 cm adds 3 instead of multiplying, and 5.3 cm divides by k."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A photo 20 cm wide and 12 cm tall is shrunk to 5 cm wide without squashing it. How tall is it now, in cm?","answer":3,"hint":"The scale factor is new ÷ old = 5 ÷ 20. It is less than 1.","explain":"k = 5 ÷ 20 = 0.25, so the height is 12 × 0.25 = 3 cm."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Triangle ADE sits inside triangle ABC, with DE parallel to BC. Which side of ABC matches AD?","choices":["AB","DB","BC"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pull ADE out. Which side of the big triangle starts at A and runs the same way as AD?","explain":"AD is part of AB, and both start at the shared angle A. DB is only the leftover piece, so it does not match."}],"explain":{"kid":["Two shapes are similar when one is a scaled copy of the other. The angles stay the same. Every side is multiplied by the same number, the scale factor k.","Find k from one pair of matching sides. If 4 cm becomes 10 cm, k = 10 ÷ 4 = 2.5. Then a 6 cm side becomes 6 × 2.5 = 15 cm.","Do not add. Going from 4 to 10 adds 6, but adding 6 to every side would change the shape. Copies always multiply.","A copy can be turned or flipped. Match sides by the angles. Each side sits opposite an angle, and matching sides sit opposite equal angles."],"adult":["Similar figures have equal matching angles, and matching lengths share one ratio: the scale factor k = image length ÷ object length. A missing length is a known length × k. It is also the answer to a proportion such as x/6 = 10/4.","Pair the sides by angles, not by where they are drawn. In a triangle, the shortest side is opposite the smallest angle. Mark the equal angles and read the side opposite each one. This still works after a flip or a turn.","Overlapping triangles are the usual exam setting. If DE is parallel to BC, triangle ADE is similar to ABC. They share angle A, and the parallel lines make equal corresponding angles. Redraw them apart: AD pairs with the whole of AB = AD + DB, and DE pairs with BC.","Common mistakes: adding the same amount to every side instead of multiplying; pairing AD with DB rather than AB; matching sides by where they are drawn; dividing by k when going from small to large; and writing a proportion with one fraction upside down. A k below 1 still multiplies; it shrinks the shape."]},"familyTip":"Take a photo and print or show it at two sizes. Measure the width and height of each. Divide new by old for both. Are the two answers the same? Now try squashing the image in an editor: the two answers no longer match, and the picture looks wrong.","keyIdea":"Similar shapes are the same shape at different sizes, so every length is multiplied by one scale factor k = new length ÷ matching old length. Match sides by the equal angles they sit opposite, pull overlapping triangles apart first, then multiply by k or solve a proportion.","worked":{"title":"DE is parallel to BC. AD = 3 cm, DB = 5 cm and DE = 4.5 cm. Find BC.","steps":["Pull the triangles apart: ADE is similar to ABC, sharing the angle at A.","AD matches the whole of AB = 3 + 5 = 8 cm, not DB.","Scale factor from ADE to ABC: k = 8 ÷ 3.","BC = 4.5 × 8 ÷ 3 = 12 cm. Check: 3/8 = 4.5/12 = 0.375."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which triangles are similar to one with sides 4, 6 and 8 cm? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Similar to 4, 6, 8","Not similar"],"items":[["2, 3, 4",0],["8, 12, 16",0],["6, 9, 12",0],["6, 8, 10",1],["5, 7, 9",1],["4, 6, 9",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagsim","caption":"Flagship lab: stretch a triangle by a scale factor, match sides by their angles, pull overlapping triangles apart, slide a parallel cut and shrink a photo.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_I1uIFFoUsb":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why is opposite ÷ hypotenuse the same in every right triangle with a 35° angle?","choices":["They all have angles 35°, 90° and 55°, so they are similar","They all have the same hypotenuse","The opposite side is always 0.574 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"What do the angles of these triangles have in common?","explain":"Equal angles make the triangles similar. Similar triangles multiply every side by the same factor, so ratios of sides do not change."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"sin 35° ≈ 0.574. A right triangle with a 35° angle has hypotenuse 20 cm. How long is the side opposite the 35° angle, to 1 decimal place?","answer":11.5,"tolerance":0.05,"hint":"opposite ÷ hypotenuse = 0.574, so opposite = 20 × 0.574.","explain":"20 × 0.574 = 11.48, which is 11.5 cm to 1 decimal place."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Triangle A has opposite 3 and hypotenuse 5. Triangle B has the same angle θ and hypotenuse 15. Which is true?","choices":["B has opposite 9, and both have sin θ = 0.6","B has a bigger sin θ because it is bigger","B has opposite 13"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find the scale factor 15 ÷ 5, then multiply.","explain":"k = 3, so B's opposite side is 9. Then 9 ÷ 15 = 3 ÷ 5 = 0.6. Size does not change sin θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"In triangle PQR the right angle is at R and θ is at Q. What is cos θ?","choices":["QR/PQ","PR/PQ","QR/PR"],"answer":0,"hint":"Hypotenuse: opposite the right angle. Adjacent: touches Q and is not the hypotenuse.","explain":"PQ is the hypotenuse and QR is adjacent to θ, so cos θ = QR/PQ. PR/PQ is sin θ."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m5","prompt":"A right triangle has opposite {=3*k}, adjacent {=4*k} and hypotenuse {=5*k}. What is sin θ as a decimal?","answer":"3*k/(5*k)","hint":"sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = {=3*k} ÷ {=5*k}.","explain":"{=3*k} ÷ {=5*k} = 0.6, the same as 3 ÷ 5. The {k} cancels, whatever size the triangle is.","params":{"k":[1,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Kai says: 'sin 20° ≈ 0.342, so sin 40° ≈ 0.684.' Is he right?","choices":["No: sin 40° ≈ 0.643, because sine is not proportional to the angle","Yes: double the angle, double the sine","No: sin 40° is 0.342 as well"],"answer":0,"hint":"Draw both triangles with hypotenuse 10. Does the opposite side double?","explain":"sin 40° ≈ 0.643. The ratio grows with the angle, but more and more slowly, so doubling θ does not double sin θ."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which of these can never be bigger than 1?","choices":["sin θ, since the hypotenuse is the longest side","tan θ, since it uses the two shorter sides","Neither of them can be bigger than 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which ratio has the longest side on the bottom?","explain":"sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse, and the hypotenuse is longest, so sin θ < 1. tan 60° ≈ 1.73, so tan can be bigger than 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Two right triangles share an angle θ. The small one has adjacent {a} cm and hypotenuse {h} cm. The big one has adjacent {=a*k} cm. How long is its hypotenuse?","answer":"h*k","hint":"Scale factor = {=a*k} ÷ {a}. Multiply the hypotenuse by it.","explain":"k = {k}, so the hypotenuse is {h} × {k} = {=h*k} cm, and cos θ stays {a}/{h}.","params":{"a":[2,6],"h":[7,12],"k":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"Two right triangles share an angle θ. The small one has opposite {o} cm and hypotenuse {h} cm. The big one has hypotenuse {=h*k} cm. How long is its opposite side?","answer":"o*k","hint":"The triangles are similar. The scale factor is {=h*k} ÷ {h}.","explain":"k = {k}, so the opposite side is {o} × {k} = {=o*k} cm. The ratio opposite ÷ hypotenuse stays {o}/{h}.","params":{"o":[2,6],"h":[7,12],"k":[2,4]},"where":"o<h"},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"A right triangle has a 40° angle. What is its third angle?","choices":["50°","40°","140°"],"answer":0,"hint":"The three angles add up to 180°, and one of them is 90°.","explain":"180 − 90 − 40 = 50°. Every right triangle with a 40° angle has angles 40°, 50° and 90°, so they are all similar."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"In a right triangle, opposite = {o} and hypotenuse = {h}. A similar triangle is {k} times as big. What is opposite ÷ hypotenuse in the big triangle? Give a decimal.","answer":"o/h","hint":"Both sides are multiplied by {k}. What happens to the fraction?","explain":"({o} × {k}) ÷ ({h} × {k}) = {o} ÷ {h} = {=o/h}. The {k} cancels, so the ratio is unchanged.","params":{"o":[1,4],"h":[5,10],"k":[2,5]},"where":"(100*o)%h==0"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"In triangle PQR the right angle is at Q and θ is at P. Which side is opposite θ?","choices":["QR","PQ","PR"],"answer":0,"hint":"The opposite side is the one that does not touch the corner P.","explain":"QR does not touch P, so it is opposite θ. PR is opposite the right angle, so it is the hypotenuse, and PQ is adjacent."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"sin 30° = 0.5. What is sin 60°?","choices":["About 0.87","1","0.25"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is sin θ proportional to θ? Try it on a calculator.","explain":"sin 60° ≈ 0.866. Doubling the angle does not double the ratio, and sin θ can never be more than 1."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw two right triangles with a 35° angle, one small and one big. Both have a third angle of 55°, because the angles add up to 180°.","Same angles means same shape. The big one is a scaled copy of the small one. Every side is multiplied by the same number.","So the fraction opposite ÷ hypotenuse is the same in both. If the big one is twice as big, the top and the bottom both double. 2.87 ÷ 5 and 5.74 ÷ 10 both give 0.574.","That number belongs to the angle, not to the triangle. We call it sin 35°. A calculator knows it for every angle."],"adult":["Two right triangles that share an acute angle θ have all three angles equal: θ, 90° and 90° − θ. So they are similar (AA). Matching sides share one scale factor k, and a ratio of two sides in one triangle does not change when both are multiplied by k.","That is why sin θ = opposite/hypotenuse, cos θ = adjacent/hypotenuse and tan θ = opposite/adjacent are well defined. Each one depends on θ alone. A calculator's sin 35° ≈ 0.574 is that shared ratio, for a triangle of any size.","The names come from where θ is. The hypotenuse is opposite the right angle. The opposite side does not touch θ. The adjacent side touches θ and is not the hypotenuse. Turn the triangle and the names move with θ.","Common mistakes: thinking a bigger triangle has a bigger sine; treating sin θ as proportional to θ (sin 60° ≈ 0.866, not 2 × sin 30° = 1); calling the bottom side adjacent whichever way the triangle faces; writing a ratio upside down, which would make sin or cos bigger than 1; and reading 0.574 as a length instead of a ratio."]},"familyTip":"With a protractor, draw two right triangles that both have a 35° angle, one small and one as big as the page. Measure the side opposite the 35° angle and the longest side of each, then divide. Do both give about 0.57? Check sin 35 on a calculator.","keyIdea":"Every right triangle with the same acute angle θ has the same three angles, θ, 90° and 90° − θ, so they are all similar. Similar triangles keep their side ratios, so opposite ÷ hypotenuse, adjacent ÷ hypotenuse and opposite ÷ adjacent depend only on θ. Those fixed numbers are sin θ, cos θ and tan θ.","worked":{"title":"Show that sin 35° is the same in right triangles with hypotenuse 5 cm and 12 cm.","steps":["Both triangles have angles 35°, 90° and 55°, so they are similar.","Scale factor from the small to the big triangle: k = 12 ÷ 5 = 2.4.","The small opposite side is about 2.868 cm, so the big one is 2.868 × 2.4 ≈ 6.883 cm.","Ratios: 2.868 ÷ 5 ≈ 0.574 and 6.883 ÷ 12 ≈ 0.574. Both equal sin 35°."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which changes make sin θ change? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Changes sin θ","Leaves sin θ the same"],"items":[["Make θ bigger",0],["Make θ smaller",0],["Move θ to the other sharp corner",0],["Double every side",1],["Halve every side",1],["Flip the triangle over",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtrig","caption":"Flagship lab: resize a 35° right triangle, cut similar copies from it, name the sides of a turned triangle and swing the angle to see what sin θ depends on.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zepH6Dsn_G":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is sin 45° in exact form?","choices":["√2/2","1/2","√2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Half a unit square has legs 1 and 1 and hypotenuse √2.","explain":"sin 45° = 1/√2 = √2/2 ≈ 0.707. 1/2 treats 45° as half of 90° in value, and √2 is hypotenuse ÷ leg."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"What is tan 90°?","choices":["1","0","Undefined: cos 90° = 0, so it would divide by 0"],"answer":2,"hint":"tan = sin ÷ cos. What is cos 90°?","explain":"At 90° the adjacent side shrinks to 0, so tan 90° = 1 ÷ 0, which has no value. tan 45° is the one equal to 1."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"A right triangle has hypotenuse {d} cm and an angle of 60°. How long is the side next to the 60° angle (not the hypotenuse), in cm?","answer":"d/2","hint":"The side next to 60° is adjacent, so use cos 60° = 1/2.","explain":"Adjacent = {d} × cos 60° = {d} × 1/2 = {=d/2} cm.","params":{"d":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Which triangle shows that sin 60° = √3/2?","choices":["Half of an equilateral triangle with side 2","Half of a square with side 1","A 3, 4, 5 triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"You need angles of 30°, 60° and 90°.","explain":"Halving an equilateral triangle of side 2 gives sides 1, √3, 2 and angles 30°, 60°, 90°. Half a square gives 45°, and a 3, 4, 5 triangle has no 60° angle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Ana says sin 60° = 2 × sin 30° = 1. What went wrong?","choices":["Sine is not proportional to the angle: sin 60° = √3/2 ≈ 0.87","She should have used 3 × sin 30°","Nothing, sin 60° is 1"],"answer":0,"hint":"Doubling the angle does not double the side opposite it.","explain":"sin 60° = √3/2 ≈ 0.866, not 1. Only sin 90° = 1. The sine curve bends, so it is not a straight line through the origin."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Without a calculator, work out sin 30° + cos 60°.","answer":1,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"Both come from the half equilateral triangle. Which side is opposite 30° and which is adjacent to 60°?","explain":"sin 30° = 1/2 and cos 60° = 1/2, the same side 1 over the hypotenuse 2. So the sum is 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Half an equilateral triangle has hypotenuse 2 and base 1. What is its exact height?","choices":["√3","1","√5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pythagoras: height² = 2² − 1².","explain":"height² = 4 − 1 = 3, so the height is √3 ≈ 1.73. 1 comes from 2 − 1 without squaring, and √5 adds the squares instead of subtracting."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"Work out (sin 60°)² without a calculator. Give your answer as a decimal.","answer":0.75,"tolerance":0.001,"hint":"sin 60° = √3/2. Square the top and the bottom.","explain":"(√3/2)² = 3/4 = 0.75. Check: cos 60° = 1/2, and 3/4 + 1/4 = 1, as sin² + cos² = 1."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"What is cos 0°?","choices":["0","1","Undefined"],"answer":1,"hint":"Picture a hypotenuse of 1 lying flat on the ground. How long is the base?","explain":"At 0° the hypotenuse lies along the ground, so the base is the whole length 1 and the height is 0: cos 0° = 1 and sin 0° = 0."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m10","prompt":"A straight ramp rises at 30° to the ground and its top is {k} m high. How long is the ramp, in metres?","answer":"2*k","hint":"The height is opposite 30°, so height = ramp × sin 30° = ramp × 1/2. Work backwards.","explain":"{k} = ramp × 1/2, so the ramp is 2 × {k} = {=2*k} m. Halving the height instead would make the ramp shorter than its own height.","params":{"k":[1,12]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"numeric","id":"p1","prompt":"A ladder {d} m long leans at 30° to the ground. How high up the wall does it reach, in metres?","answer":"d/2","hint":"The height is opposite the 30° angle, so height = {d} × sin 30°, and sin 30° = 1/2.","explain":"Height = {d} × sin 30° = {d} × 1/2 = {=d/2} m.","params":{"d":[2,15]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"What is the exact value of cos 30°?","choices":["√3/2","1/2","√3"],"answer":0,"hint":"In the half triangle with sides 1, √3, 2, which side touches the 30° corner but is not the hypotenuse?","explain":"The √3 side is adjacent to 30°, and the hypotenuse is 2, so cos 30° = √3/2. 1/2 is sin 30°, and √3 drops the ÷ 2."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A right triangle has a 45° angle. The side opposite it is {k} cm. How long is the side adjacent to it, in cm?","answer":"k","hint":"tan 45° = opposite ÷ adjacent = 1, so the two shorter sides are equal.","explain":"tan 45° = 1, so adjacent = opposite = {k} cm. It is half of a square.","params":{"k":[2,20]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which is the exact value of tan 30°?","choices":["√3","√3/3","1/2"],"answer":1,"hint":"tan = opposite ÷ adjacent. Opposite 30° is 1 and adjacent is √3.","explain":"tan 30° = 1/√3. Multiply top and bottom by √3 to get √3/3 ≈ 0.577. √3 is tan 60°, and 1/2 is sin 30°."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"A right triangle has hypotenuse 10 cm and an angle of 60°. What is the exact length of the side opposite 60°?","choices":["5√3 cm","5 cm","10√3 cm"],"answer":0,"hint":"Opposite = hypotenuse × sin 60°, and sin 60° = √3/2.","explain":"10 × √3/2 = 5√3 ≈ 8.66 cm. 5 cm is the side next to 60° (10 × cos 60°), and 10√3 is longer than the hypotenuse."}],"explain":{"kid":["Draw a triangle with all three sides 2. Fold it in half from the top. Each half has angles of 30°, 60° and 90°.","The bottom of each half is 1. The long side is still 2. To find the height, square and take away: 2 × 2 − 1 × 1 = 3. So the height is √3.","Now read the sides. Sin of an angle is the side across from it over the long side. Across from 30° is 1, so sin 30° is 1/2. Across from 60° is √3, so sin 60° is √3/2.","Cut a square of side 1 from corner to corner. Both small angles are 45°, and the cut is √2 long. Tan 45° is 1 ÷ 1, which is 1."],"adult":["Halve an equilateral triangle of side 2. The half has hypotenuse 2 and short side 1. By Pythagoras its height is √(4 − 1) = √3. The 30° angle faces the side 1, so sin 30° = 1/2, cos 30° = √3/2 and tan 30° = 1/√3 = √3/3.","The 60° angle swaps opposite and adjacent, so sin 60° = √3/2, cos 60° = 1/2 and tan 60° = √3. Cutting a unit square on its diagonal gives legs 1 and 1 and hypotenuse √2. So sin 45° = cos 45° = 1/√2 = √2/2, and tan 45° = 1.","At the ends, a hypotenuse of 1 lies flat at 0° and stands up at 90°. So sin 0° = 0, cos 0° = 1, tan 0° = 0, sin 90° = 1 and cos 90° = 0. tan 90° is undefined, because it would mean dividing by 0. Exact values keep answers exact: a 6 m ladder at 60° reaches 6 × √3/2 = 3√3 m, not a rounded 5.196 m. A memory aid: the sines of 0°, 30°, 45°, 60° and 90° are √0/2, √1/2, √2/2, √3/2 and √4/2. The cosines are the same list in reverse.","Common mistakes: thinking sin 60° is double sin 30°; swapping sin and cos for 30° and 60°; writing tan 30° as √3, which is tan 60°; dropping the ÷ 2 so that sin 60° becomes √3; finding the height as 2 − 1 instead of √(2² − 1²); and giving tan 90° a value."]},"familyTip":"Cut a paper square in half along its diagonal and fold a paper equilateral triangle in half. Label the sides 1, 1, √2 and 1, √3, 2. Then quiz each other: which side is across from 30°? What is sin 30°?","keyIdea":"Two special triangles give exact values with no calculator. Halve an equilateral triangle of side 2 to get sides 1, √3 and 2 with angles 30°, 60° and 90°. Cut a unit square along its diagonal to get sides 1, 1 and √2 with two 45° angles. Read sin, cos and tan off the sides: sin 30° = 1/2, sin 45° = √2/2, sin 60° = √3/2, tan 45° = 1, tan 60° = √3.","worked":{"title":"A ramp is 8 m long and rises at 30° to the ground. Find its exact height and the exact distance it covers along the ground.","steps":["The ramp is the hypotenuse, 8 m. The height is opposite 30°, so height = 8 × sin 30°.","sin 30° = 1/2 from the half equilateral triangle, so height = 8 × 1/2 = 4 m.","The ground distance is adjacent to 30°, so it is 8 × cos 30° = 8 × √3/2 = 4√3 m.","Check with Pythagoras: 4² + (4√3)² = 16 + 48 = 64 = 8². 4√3 ≈ 6.93 m."]},"visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Which exact value is each one? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["1/2","√2/2","√3/2"],"items":[["sin 30°",0],["cos 60°",0],["sin 45°",1],["cos 45°",1],["sin 60°",2],["cos 30°",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagexact","caption":"Flagship lab: fold an equilateral triangle and cut a square to find exact sin, cos and tan, then use them for surd lengths.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qaf61_4L_L":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What does sin²θ mean?","choices":["(sin θ)²","sin(θ²)","2 sin θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"The small 2 belongs to the whole ratio.","explain":"sin²θ is shorthand for (sin θ)², the sine of θ multiplied by itself."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"cos θ = 0.6. Which is true?","choices":["sin θ = 0.8 or −0.8, depending on the quadrant","sin θ = 0.4","sin θ = 0.8 in every quadrant"],"answer":0,"hint":"sin²θ = 1 − 0.36. How many square roots does 0.64 have?","explain":"sin²θ = 0.64, so sin θ = ±0.8. Above the x-axis it is 0.8; below it is −0.8."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Simplify (sin θ + cos θ)².","choices":["1 + 2 sin θ cos θ","1","sin θ + cos θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Expand the bracket before using the identity.","explain":"(sin θ + cos θ)² = sin²θ + 2 sin θ cos θ + cos²θ = 1 + 2 sin θ cos θ."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"cos θ = 1/{k}. Find tan²θ + 1.","answer":"k*k","params":{"k":[2,9]},"hint":"Divide sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 by cos²θ.","explain":"tan²θ + 1 = 1/cos²θ = 1 ÷ (1/{k})² = {=k*k}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"sin θ = −8/17 and θ is in quadrant IV. What is cos θ?","choices":["15/17","−15/17","9/17"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos²θ = 1 − 64/289. In quadrant IV, is x positive or negative?","explain":"cos²θ = 225/289, so cos θ = ±15/17. In quadrant IV, x is positive, so cos θ = 15/17."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"Find {k} − {k} sin²({a}°) − {k} cos²({a}°).","answer":"0*k","params":{"k":[2,9],"a":[1,359]},"hint":"Group the two squared terms and take out {k}.","explain":"{k} − {k}(sin²({a}°) + cos²({a}°)) = {k} − {k} × 1 = {=0*k}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"A student writes: sin θ = 0.6, so cos θ = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4. What went wrong?","choices":["The identity is about squares: cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ","Nothing; the working is right","They should have added: cos θ = 1.6"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the identity sin θ + cos θ = 1?","explain":"sin θ + cos θ is not 1. Use squares: cos²θ = 1 − 0.36 = 0.64, so cos θ = ±0.8."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m8","prompt":"cos θ = {c}/10 with θ in quadrant I. Find sin²θ as a decimal.","answer":"(100-c*c)/100","params":{"c":[1,9]},"hint":"sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ. Square {c}/10 first.","explain":"sin²θ = 1 − ({c}/10)² = 1 − {=c*c/100} = {=(100-c*c)/100}."},{"type":"mc","id":"m9","prompt":"Rewrite 2 sin²x + 3 cos x = 3 using only cos x.","choices":["2 − 2cos²x + 3 cos x = 3","2 + 3 cos x = 3","2 sin x + 3 cos x = 3"],"answer":0,"hint":"Replace sin²x with 1 − cos²x.","explain":"sin²x = 1 − cos²x, so 2 sin²x = 2 − 2cos²x, giving 2 − 2cos²x + 3 cos x = 3, a quadratic in cos x."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Why is sin²θ + cos²θ = 1 for every angle θ?","choices":["The point (cos θ, sin θ) on the unit circle is 1 from the origin","sin θ and cos θ are always equal to 0.5","It is only true when θ is acute"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where is the point (cos θ, sin θ), and how far is it from the centre?","explain":"The point lies on x² + y² = 1, so its coordinates satisfy cos²θ + sin²θ = 1. That is true in every quadrant."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"sin θ = {s}/10. Find cos²θ as a decimal.","answer":"(100-s*s)/100","params":{"s":[1,9]},"hint":"Use cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ. Square sin θ first.","explain":"cos²θ = 1 − ({s}/10)² = 1 − {=s*s/100} = {=(100-s*s)/100}."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"sin θ = 3/5 and θ is in quadrant II. What is cos θ?","choices":["−4/5","4/5","2/5"],"answer":0,"hint":"Find cos²θ, then use the quadrant to choose the sign.","explain":"cos²θ = 1 − 9/25 = 16/25, so cos θ = ±4/5. In quadrant II, x is negative, so cos θ = −4/5."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"Without a calculator, find {k} sin²({a}°) + {k} cos²({a}°).","answer":"k","params":{"k":[2,9],"a":[1,359]},"hint":"Take out the common factor first.","explain":"{k}(sin²({a}°) + cos²({a}°)) = {k} × 1 = {=k}, whatever the angle."},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Simplify (1 − cos²θ) / sin θ.","choices":["sin θ","1","cos θ"],"answer":0,"hint":"Rewrite 1 − cos²θ using the identity.","explain":"1 − cos²θ = sin²θ, and sin²θ / sin θ = sin θ."}],"explain":{"kid":["Take a circle with radius 1. Turn a radius by an angle θ. Its end point is at (cos θ, sin θ). Drop a line straight down from that point. Now you have a right triangle. Its long side is 1. Its short sides are cos θ across and sin θ up.","Pythagoras says the two short sides, squared, add up to the long side squared. So cos²θ + sin²θ = 1² = 1. Here sin²θ means sin θ times sin θ. It is not sin of θ squared.","Turn the radius past 90°. Now cos θ or sin θ can be less than zero. But a square is never less than zero, and the point is still 1 from the centre. So the rule holds for every angle you pick.","The rule helps you find one ratio from the other. Say sin θ = 0.6. Then cos²θ = 1 − 0.36 = 0.64, so cos θ is 0.8 or −0.8. The quadrant tells you which one. In quadrant II, cos θ is −0.8. The rule also tidies up messy sums. Where you see 1 − cos²θ, write sin²θ. Where you see sin²θ + cos²θ, write 1."],"adult":["This topic, for ages 16 to 18, proves and uses the Pythagorean identity sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. The proof is short. On the unit circle the point at angle θ is (cos θ, sin θ). It lies on x² + y² = 1, so cos²θ + sin²θ = 1. For an acute angle this is Pythagoras in the triangle with hypotenuse 1. For other angles the signs change but the squares do not, so it holds for all θ.","Rearranged, it gives sin²θ = 1 − cos²θ and cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ. Taking a square root gives two values, so the quadrant must pick the sign. If sin θ = 5/13 and θ is obtuse, then cos θ = −12/13. Dividing the identity by cos²θ gives tan²θ + 1 = 1/cos²θ, a useful second form.","In simplifying, spot the pattern and swap. (1 − cos²θ)/sin θ becomes sin²θ/sin θ = sin θ. (sin θ + cos θ)² expands to 1 + 2 sin θ cos θ. In equations, the identity turns a mix of sines and cosines into one function, such as a quadratic in cos θ.","Common mistakes: reading sin²θ as sin(θ²); taking cos θ = 1 − sin θ instead of using the squares; dropping the negative root, so the quadrant is ignored; and writing (sin θ + cos θ)² as sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. A quick check is to substitute an angle such as 40° into both sides."]},"familyTip":"Pick any angle and type sin(θ)² + cos(θ)² into a calculator. Try 17°, 140° and 300°. Then ask why the answer never changes, and what that has to do with a circle of radius 1.","keyIdea":"For every angle θ, sin²θ + cos²θ = 1. It is Pythagoras on the unit circle: the point (cos θ, sin θ) is 1 from the origin. Rearranged as cos²θ = 1 − sin²θ, it turns one ratio into the other, with the quadrant fixing the sign, and it simplifies expressions.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Sort each expression by what it simplifies to, using sin²θ + cos²θ = 1.","opts":{"bins":["cos²θ","sin²θ","1"],"items":[["1 − sin²θ",0],["(1 − sin θ)(1 + sin θ)",0],["1 − cos²θ",1],["(1 − cos θ)(1 + cos θ)",1],["sin²θ + cos²θ",2],["cos²50° + sin²50°",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpyid","caption":"Flagship lab: turn the unit-circle point and watch the squares on its legs always fill 1, then pick signs by quadrant and simplify.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oM7XSZb9pI":{"mastery":{"passMark":4,"draw":5,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"What is the pole in polar coordinates?","choices":["The fixed point that r is measured from","The point where θ = π/2","The end of the initial line"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where do you start before you turn and walk?","explain":"The pole O is the fixed starting point. r is the distance from it, and the initial line starts there."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Which polar point is the same as (−{a}, π/6)?","choices":["({a}, 7π/6)","({a}, π/6)","({a}, −π/6)"],"answer":0,"hint":"A negative r walks backwards. Which turn faces that way?","explain":"(−{a}, π/6) faces π/6 and walks back {a}, which is the same as turning an extra π: ({a}, 7π/6).","params":{"a":[2,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m3","prompt":"The polar point ({a}, 3π/2) has which Cartesian y-coordinate?","answer":"-a","hint":"3π/2 is three quarters of a turn. Which way does that point?","explain":"y = {a} × sin(3π/2) = {a} × (−1) = −{a}. Three quarters of a turn points straight down.","params":{"a":[1,7]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Why does a point have many polar names but only one Cartesian name?","choices":["Turning a full 2π more lands on the same ray","Because r can be any size","Because radians are not exact"],"answer":0,"hint":"What happens if you turn one extra full lap?","explain":"(r, θ) and (r, θ + 2π) are the same point, so you can add or take away any number of full turns."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which polar point is the Cartesian point (0, {a})?","choices":["({a}, π/2)","({a}, 0)","(0, {a})"],"answer":0,"hint":"The point is straight up, {a} from the pole.","explain":"Straight up is a quarter turn, π/2, and the distance is {a}, so ({a}, π/2). (0, {a}) in polar would be the pole.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Sam plots (2, π/3) by turning π/3 clockwise from straight up. What went wrong?","choices":["He used bearings: θ is anticlockwise from the initial line","Nothing, that is correct","He should have walked 3 units"],"answer":0,"hint":"Where does θ start, and which way does it turn?","explain":"Polar angles start on the initial line, pointing right, and turn anticlockwise. Sam measured like a bearing."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Which polar coordinates describe the pole?","choices":["(0, θ) for any θ","Only (0, 0)","(1, 0)"],"answer":0,"hint":"If r = 0, does the turn matter?","explain":"With r = 0 you never walk away from O, so every angle gives the pole."},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Is (4, π/4 + π) the same point as (4, π/4)?","choices":["No, it is on the opposite side of the pole","Yes, adding π is a full turn","Yes, because r is the same"],"answer":0,"hint":"A full turn is 2π, not π.","explain":"Adding π is a half turn, so the ray points the opposite way. Only 2π comes back to the same point."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"In polar coordinates, where is θ measured from, and which way?","choices":["From the initial line, anticlockwise","From straight up, clockwise","From the initial line, clockwise"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think of the ray that points right from the pole.","explain":"θ starts on the initial line and positive θ turns anticlockwise. Clockwise from straight up is how bearings work, not polar angles."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"The polar point ({a}, π) has which Cartesian x-coordinate?","answer":"-a","hint":"Use x = r cos θ, and cos π = −1.","explain":"x = {a} × cos π = {a} × (−1) = −{a}. A half turn points left of the pole.","params":{"a":[2,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which is another name for the point ({a}, π/3)?","choices":["({a}, 7π/3)","({a}, 4π/3)","({a}, −π/3)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which change is a full turn?","explain":"7π/3 = π/3 + 2π, a full extra lap, so ({a}, 7π/3) is the same point. Adding π points the other way, and −π/3 is the mirror image.","params":{"a":[2,6]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"The polar point ({a}, π/2) has which Cartesian y-coordinate?","answer":"a","hint":"Use y = r sin θ, and sin(π/2) = 1.","explain":"y = {a} × sin(π/2) = {a}. A quarter turn points straight up, so the point is {a} units above the pole.","params":{"a":[1,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p5","prompt":"Where is the point (3, 2π/3)?","choices":["Up and to the left of the pole","Up and to the right of the pole","Down and to the left of the pole"],"answer":0,"hint":"π/2 is straight up. Is 2π/3 more or less than π/2?","explain":"2π/3 is more than π/2 but less than π, so the ray points up and to the left."}],"explain":{"kid":["Polar coordinates find a point by a turn and a walk. Start at the pole, O. Face along the initial line, which points right. Turn by θ, then walk r units out. So (3, π/2) means a quarter turn, then 3 steps: straight up.","θ is in radians and it turns anticlockwise. A half turn is π and a full turn is 2π. So 2π/3 is past straight up: the point (3, 2π/3) sits up and to the left. A negative θ, like −π/2, turns clockwise.","One point has many names. A full extra lap brings you back to the same ray. So (3, π/4), (3, 9π/4) and (3, −7π/4) are all one point. The pole is (0, θ) for any θ, since you never walk away.","If r is negative, face along θ but walk backwards. (−2, π/3) lands on the far side of the pole, at the same place as (2, 4π/3). To get x and y, use x = r cos θ and y = r sin θ. So (4, π) is the Cartesian point (−4, 0)."],"adult":["This A-level Further Maths topic (ages 16 to 18) sets up the polar system: a fixed point O called the pole, a ray from it called the initial line, and points given as (r, θ). The angle is measured from the initial line, anticlockwise for positive θ, and in radians. The conversions are x = r cos θ, y = r sin θ and r² = x² + y².","Unlike Cartesian coordinates, polar names are not unique. (r, θ) and (r, θ + 2nπ) are the same point for any whole number n, and the pole is (0, θ) for every θ. If negative r is allowed, (−r, θ) is the same point as (r, θ + π). Exam boards often fix a principal form, such as r ≥ 0 with −π < θ ≤ π or 0 ≤ θ < 2π, so learners should check which their course uses.","Common mistakes: turning clockwise, as with bearings; measuring from the vertical instead of the initial line; reading 2π/3 as less than a right angle because it is a small number; and thinking (r, θ + π) is the same point, when it is opposite. Another slip is finding θ with tan⁻¹(y/x) and forgetting to check the quadrant.","In the lab, learners drag a point round a polar grid, plot points in three quadrants, wind extra laps to find three names for one point, walk backwards for negative r, and read x = r cos θ and y = r sin θ live. The challenge mixes plotting with naming the same point another way, with feedback on each slip."]},"familyTip":"Stand in a room facing one wall: that is the initial line. Call out a turn and a number of steps, such as \"a quarter turn left, then 3 steps\", and see where you end up. Then find another turn that ends in the same place.","keyIdea":"A polar point (r, θ) is found by turning from the initial line by θ radians anticlockwise, then walking r units out from the pole. Adding or taking away 2π gives the same point, so every point has many polar names. A negative r means walk backwards, which is the same as turning an extra π. To change to Cartesian, use x = r cos θ and y = r sin θ.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Same point as (2, π/3), or a different point? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["Same point","Different point"],"items":[["(2, 7π/3)",0],["(2, −5π/3)",0],["(−2, 4π/3)",0],["(2, 4π/3)",1],["(2, −π/3)",1],["(−2, π/3)",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagpolar","caption":"Flagship lab: turn and walk to plot polar points, wind extra laps for new names, walk backwards for negative r and read off x and y.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_zt0LlV_JjB":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Which shape has 3 corners?","choices":["Triangle","Square","Circle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count the pointy bits on each shape.","explain":"A triangle has 3 corners and 3 straight sides. A square has 4 corners and a circle has none."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"The square will not go in the round hole. Why?","choices":["It has corners","It is too red","It is upside down"],"answer":0,"hint":"What does a square have that a round hole does not?","explain":"The square has corners and straight sides. The round hole has no corners, so the square needs the square hole."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Which shape fits any way you turn it?","choices":["Circle","Triangle","Rectangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which shape looks the same however you turn it?","explain":"A circle fits any way round. It is round all the way, so there are no corners to line up."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A long rectangle is standing up. Its hole lies flat. What do you do?","choices":["Turn it to lie flat","Push it in standing up","Put it in the square hole"],"answer":0,"hint":"Make the piece look like the hole.","explain":"Turn the rectangle so it lies flat like the hole. The square hole is too short for it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Leo fits {n} round pieces in a row. Which hole did they go in?","choices":["The round hole","The star hole","The square hole"],"answer":0,"hint":"Round pieces match a hole with the same round edge.","explain":"All {n} round pieces go in the round hole. Round fits round.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which one has no corners?","choices":["An oval","A square","A triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Run your finger round the edge. Do you feel a pointy bit?","explain":"An oval is curved all the way round, like an egg, so it has no corners."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Mia puts {n} blocks on a slope. They do not roll. Why not?","choices":["They have flat sides","They are too small","The slope is round"],"answer":0,"hint":"What stops a block? Look at its sides and corners.","explain":"All {n} blocks have flat sides and corners, so they slide or stop. Round things roll.","params":{"n":[2,5]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which hole does a round circle fit?","choices":["The round hole","The square hole","The triangle hole"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for the hole with no pointy corners.","explain":"A circle fits the round hole. It has no corners, so it needs a hole with no corners."},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Ava has {n} triangles. One will not go in its hole. What should she do?","choices":["Turn it","Push harder","Throw it away"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is it the right shape? Then the corners must line up.","explain":"Turn it until the corners line up with the hole. All {n} triangles fit once they are turned the right way.","params":{"n":[2,6]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"How many corners does a square have?","choices":["4","3","None"],"answer":0,"hint":"Touch each pointy bit and count.","explain":"A square has 4 corners and 4 straight sides. A triangle has 3 corners, and a circle has none."},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Sam puts {n} shapes on a slope. Which one rolls away?","choices":["The ball","The block","The triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which shape is round all the way, with no flat side to stop it?","explain":"The ball rolls. Of Sam's {n} shapes, only the round one has no flat side or corner to stop it.","params":{"n":[3,7]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Look at the shape. Now look at the holes. Find the hole that looks the same. Round goes in round. Square goes in square.","Bump! The triangle will not go in. Do not push. Turn it round and round. When the corners line up, it drops in. Plop!","A corner is a pointy bit. A square has 4 corners. A triangle has 3. A circle has none. It is round all the way.","Round things roll. Put a ball on a slope and it rolls away. A block has flat sides, so it stops."],"adult":["Fitting pieces into a shape sorter or inset puzzle is one of the first geometry tasks for two and three year olds. To do it, a child must match the outline of a piece to the outline of a hole, notice straight edges, corners and curves, and turn the piece until it lines up. This early spatial thinking supports later work on naming 2D shapes and their properties.","At first most toddlers use trial and error: they push a piece at every hole. With practice they start to look before they try, pick the matching hole, and rotate the piece in the hand before it gets there. You can help by naming what they feel: straight side, pointy corner, round edge.","Common mistakes to watch for: pushing harder instead of turning the piece; trying a square in the rectangle hole because both have four corners; and thinking a turned triangle is a different shape. Show that it is the same triangle, just turned. A circle fits any way round, a square fits four ways, and a triangle three ways, because of how their corners repeat.","In the lab your child drags a circle, a square and a triangle into a sorter, turns an upside-down triangle until it drops in, turns a standing rectangle so it lies flat, lets shapes go on a slope to see which one rolls, sorts shapes into corners and no corners, and fills the sorter with turned pieces."]},"familyTip":"Use a real shape sorter or cut shapes from a cereal box and draw round them to make holes. When a piece will not go in, say \"Let's turn it\" rather than doing it for them. At bath time, ask which toys roll and which slide.","keyIdea":"Each shape fits the hole that matches it: round in round, square in square, triangle in triangle. If a shape is the right one but will not go in, turn it until its straight sides and corners line up with the hole. A circle has no corners, so it fits any way round, and it is the shape that rolls.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Corners or no corners? Drag each shape, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["📐 Has corners","⭕ No corners"],"items":[["Square",0],["Triangle",0],["Rectangle",0],["Diamond",0],["Circle",1],["Oval",1],["Ball",1],["Plate",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagfit","caption":"Flagship lab: match shapes to holes, turn pieces until they fit, roll shapes down a slope and sort corners from curves.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_qRlLrCnazx":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"cos(A − B) = ?","choices":["cos A cos B + sin A sin B","cos A cos B − sin A sin B","cos A − cos B"],"answer":0,"hint":"Cosine flips the sign of the angle operation.","explain":"cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B. With cos(A + B) the middle sign is minus; with A − B it becomes plus."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Expand cos({k}° − x).","choices":["cos {k}° cos x + sin {k}° sin x","cos {k}° cos x − sin {k}° sin x","cos {k}° − cos x"],"answer":0,"hint":"cos(A − B) = cos A cos B + sin A sin B, with A = {k}° and B = x.","explain":"cos({k}° − x) = cos {k}° cos x + sin {k}° sin x. For cosine the sign flips: minus inside gives plus in the middle.","params":{"k":[10,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"Solve sin 2x = cos x for 0° < x < 90°.","choices":["x = 30°","x = 45°","x = 60°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write sin 2x = 2 sin x cos x and factorise; do not divide by cos x blindly.","explain":"2 sin x cos x − cos x = cos x(2 sin x − 1) = 0. In 0° < x < 90°, cos x ≠ 0, so sin x = ½ and x = 30°."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"Simplify 2 sin {t}° cos {t}°.","choices":["sin {=2*t}°","2 sin {t}°","cos {=2*t}°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Read sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A backwards.","explain":"2 sin {t}° cos {t}° = sin(2 × {t}°) = sin {=2*t}°. Read sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A backwards.","params":{"t":[5,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Which is NOT equal to cos 2A?","choices":["2 cos A","1 − 2sin²A","cos²A − sin²A"],"answer":0,"hint":"Test A = 60°: cos 120° = −½.","explain":"2 cos 60° = 1, but cos 120° = −½, so 2 cos A is wrong. The other two are true forms of cos 2A."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"tan(A + B) = ?","choices":["(tan A + tan B)/(1 − tan A tan B)","(tan A + tan B)/(1 + tan A tan B)","tan A + tan B"],"answer":0,"hint":"Divide sin(A + B) by cos(A + B), then divide top and bottom by cos A cos B.","explain":"The bottom comes from cos(A + B), which has a minus, so tan(A + B) = (tan A + tan B)/(1 − tan A tan B)."},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"Prove-style step: cos⁴x − sin⁴x simplifies to?","choices":["cos 2x","1","cos 4x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Difference of two squares, then sin²x + cos²x = 1.","explain":"cos⁴x − sin⁴x = (cos²x − sin²x)(cos²x + sin²x) = (cos 2x)(1) = cos 2x."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Expand sin(x + {k}°).","choices":["sin x cos {k}° + cos x sin {k}°","sin x + sin {k}°","sin x cos {k}° − cos x sin {k}°"],"answer":0,"hint":"Use sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B with A = x.","explain":"sin(x + {k}°) = sin x cos {k}° + cos x sin {k}°. Sine does not split over the plus, and the sign stays + for sine.","params":{"k":[10,80]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p2","prompt":"Find the exact value of sin 15°.","choices":["(√6 − √2)/4","(√6 + √2)/4","√3/2 − √2/2"],"answer":0,"hint":"Write 15° = 45° − 30° and use sin(A − B).","explain":"sin 15° = sin 45° cos 30° − cos 45° sin 30° = (√2/2)(√3/2) − (√2/2)(½) = (√6 − √2)/4."},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Write sin {=2*t}° as a product of sin {t}° and cos {t}°.","choices":["2 sin {t}° cos {t}°","2 sin {t}°","sin {t}° cos {t}°"],"answer":0,"hint":"sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A, with A = {t}°.","explain":"sin {=2*t}° = sin(2 × {t}°) = 2 sin {t}° cos {t}°. Doubling the angle does not double the sine, and the factor 2 is needed.","params":{"t":[10,40]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Which form of cos 2x turns 3 cos 2x + 5 cos x = 1 into a quadratic in cos x?","choices":["2cos²x − 1","1 − 2sin²x","cos²x − sin²x"],"answer":0,"hint":"Pick the form that leaves only cos x.","explain":"2cos²x − 1 gives 6cos²x + 5cos x − 4 = 0, a quadratic in cos x. The other forms leave sin x in the equation."}],"explain":{"kid":["It is tempting to think sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B. Test it: A = 30° and B = 60° give sin 90° = 1. But sin 30° + sin 60° is about 1.37. No sine can be more than 1, so the rule is false.","The true rule is sin(A + B) = sin A cos B + cos A sin B. On a unit circle, the height of the point at angle A + B splits into two parts. One part is sin A cos B. The other is cos A sin B. They stack to the full height.","Cosine flips the sign: cos(A + B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B. For A − B, swap B for −B. Since cos(−B) = cos B and sin(−B) = −sin B, each sign in the middle changes. These rules give exact values, such as sin 75° = sin(45° + 30°) = (√6 + √2)/4.","Set B = A to get double angles. sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A, not 2 sin A. cos 2A = cos²A − sin²A. Swap with sin²A + cos²A = 1 to get 2cos²A − 1 or 1 − 2sin²A. Pick the form that leaves one trig function in your equation."],"adult":["These formulae sit in A-level Pure Mathematics (Edexcel, AQA and OCR, topic Trigonometry). Learners must know and use sin(A ± B), cos(A ± B) and tan(A ± B), find exact values, and use the double-angle forms to solve equations and prove identities. The formulae are in the formula book, but the uses are not.","The proof can be seen on a unit circle. Drop the point at angle A + B onto the ray at angle A. The height then splits into sin A cos B and cos A sin B. The minus sign in cos(A + B) comes from the second piece: it points back towards the y-axis. The rest follows by putting −B for B, or by dividing sin by cos for tan.","Common mistakes: splitting the function over the plus (sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B, sin 2A = 2 sin A); using a plus in cos(A + B); writing B − A in place of A − B; giving a decimal when an exact surd is asked for; picking the wrong form of cos 2A, so two trig functions remain; and dividing both sides by sin x, which loses the solutions where sin x = 0. A careful learner always factorises instead.","The lab starts with the tempting rule on a unit circle, then has the learner stack the two pieces that make the height of the A + B point. Next they fix the signs of three formulae with a live numeric check, build exact values for 15°, 75° and 105°, and pick every form of cos 2A beside a graph of sin 2A and 2 sin A."]},"keyIdea":"Sine and cosine do not split over addition: sin(A + B) is not sin A + sin B. The compound-angle formulae are sin(A ± B) = sin A cos B ± cos A sin B, cos(A ± B) = cos A cos B ∓ sin A sin B and tan(A ± B) = (tan A ± tan B)/(1 ∓ tan A tan B). Putting B = A gives the double-angle formulae: sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A, cos 2A = cos²A − sin²A = 2cos²A − 1 = 1 − 2sin²A, and tan 2A = 2 tan A/(1 − tan²A).","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Is each statement a true identity or a tempting myth? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["✅ True identity","❌ Myth"],"items":[["sin 2A = 2 sin A cos A",0],["sin 2A = 2 sin A",1],["cos(A + B) = cos A cos B − sin A sin B",0],["sin(A + B) = sin A + sin B",1],["cos 2A = 1 − 2sin²A",0],["cos(A − B) = cos A − cos B",1],["sin(A − B) = sin A cos B − cos A sin B",0],["cos 2A = 2 cos A",1]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagcomp","caption":"Flagship lab: test the tempting rule on a unit circle, stack the height of the A + B point, fix the signs, build exact values and pick every form of cos 2A.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_Ap5JD4y89P":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"Why does a ball roll off a tower?","choices":["It is round all over","It is too heavy","It is too small"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look at the outside of a ball. Is any part flat?","explain":"A ball has no flat face. It is round all over, so it rolls off."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"Where can a cone go on a tower?","choices":["Only at the very top","At the bottom","In the middle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Can a block sit on a point?","explain":"A cone has a point on top, so nothing can sit on it. It can only be the top."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"A block hangs far over the edge of the one below. What happens?","choices":["It tips off","It stays still","It gets bigger"],"answer":0,"hint":"Is the middle of the block still over the block below?","explain":"If its middle goes past the edge, it tips off. Line it up over the block below."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"The gap is 3 blocks wide. Which plank makes a bridge?","choices":["4 blocks long","3 blocks long","2 blocks long"],"answer":0,"hint":"Each end must sit on a post.","explain":"4 is longer than the gap, so both ends rest on a post. 3 just fits the gap, so it falls in."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"One post is 3 blocks tall. The other is 1 block tall. What happens to the plank?","choices":["It slopes and can slide off","It stays flat","It gets longer"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are the posts the same height?","explain":"The posts are not the same, so the plank slopes. Make both posts the same height."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"The gap is {g} blocks wide. What is the shortest plank, in whole blocks, that rests on both posts?","answer":"g+1","hint":"Just as long as the gap falls in. It must be longer.","explain":"A plank of {g} falls in. One more block, {=g+1}, is longer than the gap, so each end can rest on a post.","params":{"g":[1,5]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"A toy car is a bit taller than {n} blocks. How many blocks tall must each post be to let it under?","answer":"n+1","hint":"The posts must be taller than the car.","explain":"{n} blocks is too low, so each post needs {=n+1} blocks.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which tower is the most steady?","choices":["Wide at the bottom, thin at the top","Thin at the bottom, wide at the top","All the blocks pushed to one side"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has the big block at the bottom?","explain":"A wide bottom is steady. Thin at the bottom, or pushed to one side, will tip."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which one will stay on top of a tower?","choices":["A cube","A ball","An egg"],"answer":0,"hint":"Which one has a flat face to sit on?","explain":"A cube has flat faces, so it sits still. A ball and an egg are round, so they roll off."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"Your tower is {n} blocks tall. You put 1 more block on top. How tall is it now?","answer":"n+1","hint":"Count on one more from {n}.","explain":"{n} and 1 more is {=n+1}, so the tower is {=n+1} blocks tall.","params":{"n":[2,8]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"You have a big block and a small block. Which goes at the bottom?","choices":["The big block","The small block","It does not matter"],"answer":0,"hint":"Think about big on small. Will it tip?","explain":"The big block goes at the bottom. A wide bottom is steady. Big on small can tip over."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"A bridge has 2 posts. Each post is {n} blocks tall. Then 1 long block goes on top. How many blocks is that?","answer":"2*n+1","hint":"Count the blocks in both posts, then add the long one.","explain":"Two posts of {n} make {=2*n}, and the long block on top makes {=2*n+1}.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Some shapes stack. A cube has flat sides. A box has flat sides. Put a flat side on a flat side and it stays.","A ball is round all over. Put it on a block and it rolls off. A cone has a point on top. Nothing can sit on a point. So a cone goes last, right at the top.","Put the big block at the bottom and the small one at the top. Big on small will tip. Line each block up on the one below. If it hangs too far over the edge, it falls.","A bridge needs two posts and a long flat block on top. The block must be longer than the gap, so each end sits on a post. Make the posts the same, and taller than your car."],"adult":["This is early geometry and measures. Development Matters asks children of three and four to talk about 2D and 3D shapes using words such as flat, round, straight, sides and corners. They select shapes for a job, like flat surfaces for building and a prism for a roof. They combine shapes to make new ones, such as an arch, and compare objects by size and length. Positional words such as on top, under, between and next to belong here too.","The maths under the play: a face is a flat surface, a curved surface rolls, and a sphere has no flat face at all. A tower stays up while the middle of each block (its centre of mass) is over the block below. A wide base gives more room for that, which is why wide-bottomed towers are steadier. A bridge plank must span the gap and overlap both supports.","Common mistakes: trying to balance a ball or put a cone in the middle; starting with a small block and putting the big one on top; pushing blocks off-centre until the tower tips; choosing a plank that is just as long as the gap, so it falls in; and building pillars of different heights, so the plank slopes.","In the lab your child drags shapes onto a tower to see which ones stay, builds big-to-small towers, slides a block until its middle is over the one below, picks a plank long enough to make a bridge, and adds blocks to the pillars until a car and a bus can drive under."]},"familyTip":"Build with real blocks, boxes or tins. Before each block goes on, ask: will it stay? Then make a bridge for a toy car and ask how many blocks the posts need.","keyIdea":"Shapes with flat faces (cubes, cuboids, cylinders standing up) stack; a ball (sphere) rolls off and a cone has a point, so it can only go on top. A tower is steadier with the biggest, widest block at the bottom and each block lined up over the one below; a block tips off when its middle goes past the edge. A bridge needs two pillars the same height and a plank longer than the gap, so each end rests on a pillar; to let a toy car under, the pillars must be taller than the car.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"Good at the bottom of a tower? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["🧱 Flat faces: good at the bottom","⚽ Round or pointy: not at the bottom"],"items":[["big box",0],["ball",1],["brick",0],["orange",1],["cone",1],["thick book",0],["egg",1],["tin of beans, standing up",0]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagtower","caption":"Flagship lab: stack shapes and see which stay, build big-to-small towers, line blocks up, pick a plank for a bridge, and make the pillars tall enough for a car.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_OkN4jGLscW":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"A triangle is turned upside down. What is it now?","choices":["Still a triangle","A square","A new shape"],"answer":0,"hint":"Count its sides and corners again.","explain":"It still has 3 straight sides and 3 corners. Turning it round does not change it."},{"type":"mc","id":"m2","prompt":"A shape has 2 long sides and 2 short sides. What is it?","choices":["A rectangle","A square","A triangle"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are all 4 sides the same?","explain":"The sides are not all the same, so it is not a square. 2 long and 2 short is a rectangle."},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"How many corners does a circle have?","choices":["None","1","4"],"answer":0,"hint":"Run your finger round it. Is there a pointy bit?","explain":"A circle is round all the way, so it has no corners and no straight sides."},{"type":"mc","id":"m4","prompt":"A square stands on one corner. What is it?","choices":["Still a square","A triangle","Not a shape now"],"answer":0,"hint":"Are its 4 sides still all the same?","explain":"Its 4 sides are all still the same, so it is a square, just turned."},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"A shape has 3 straight sides, but there is a gap at the top. Is it a triangle?","choices":["No, the sides must join up","Yes, it has 3 sides","Yes, it has a corner"],"answer":0,"hint":"Do the sides go all the way round?","explain":"A triangle is closed. Its 3 sides must join up with no gap."},{"type":"mc","id":"m6","prompt":"Which one is a rectangle?","choices":["A door","A plate","Half a sandwich cut corner to corner"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for 4 sides: 2 long and 2 short.","explain":"A door has 2 long sides and 2 short sides. A plate is a circle, and the sandwich half is a triangle."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m7","prompt":"You make {n} triangles out of sticks. Each one needs 3 sticks. How many sticks do you need?","answer":"3*n","hint":"Count 3 sticks for each triangle.","explain":"{n} triangles need 3 sticks each, so {=3*n} sticks.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m8","prompt":"Which one is a triangle?","choices":["A slice of pizza with straight sides","A wheel","A window"],"answer":0,"hint":"Look for 3 straight sides and 3 corners.","explain":"Cut with straight sides, a pizza slice has 3 sides and 3 corners. A wheel is a circle and a window has 4 sides."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m9","prompt":"You draw {n} rectangles. Each one has 4 corners. How many corners is that?","answer":"4*n","hint":"Count 4 corners for each rectangle: 4, 8, 12 and so on.","explain":"{n} rectangles with 4 corners each make {=4*n} corners.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"How many corners does a square have?","choices":["4","3","0"],"answer":0,"hint":"Touch each pointy corner and count.","explain":"A square has 4 corners and 4 straight sides, all the same."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"You have {n} triangles. How many corners is that in all? Each triangle has 3.","answer":"3*n","hint":"Count 3 for each triangle: 3, 6, 9 and so on.","explain":"{n} triangles with 3 corners each make {=3*n} corners.","params":{"n":[1,4]}},{"type":"mc","id":"p3","prompt":"Which one is a circle?","choices":["A coin","An egg","A book"],"answer":0,"hint":"A circle is round the same all the way.","explain":"A coin is a circle. An egg is round but long, so it is an oval. A book has corners."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p4","prompt":"You have {n} squares. How many sides is that in all? Each square has 4.","answer":"4*n","hint":"Count 4 for each square: 4, 8 and so on.","explain":"{n} squares with 4 sides each make {=4*n} sides.","params":{"n":[1,4]}}],"explain":{"kid":["Run your finger round a shape. A straight side is like a ruler. A corner is a pointy bit where two sides meet.","A triangle has 3 sides and 3 corners. Tip it over, or make it tall and thin. It is still a triangle. But the sides must all join up and be straight.","A square has 4 sides, all the same. Stand it on a corner and it is still a square. A rectangle has 4 sides too: 2 long and 2 short. A door is a rectangle.","A circle is round all the way, like a plate or a clock. It has no corners. An egg is round too, but it is long. That shape is an oval."],"adult":["This is early shape and space. Development Matters asks three and four year olds to talk about 2D shapes such as circles, rectangles and triangles. They use words like sides, corners, straight, flat and round. In Reception they turn and move shapes. In Year 1 they name common 2D shapes in different sizes and turned different ways.","What matters is the rule, not the picture. A triangle is any closed shape with 3 straight sides. A square has 4 equal sides and 4 square corners (right angles). A rectangle has 4 square corners, and its opposite sides are equal. Strictly, a square is a special rectangle, but at this age children tell them apart by side length. A circle has one curved edge and no corners (vertices).","Common mistakes: calling a square on its point a diamond, as if it were a new shape; thinking only an upright triangle with equal sides counts; calling every 4-sided shape a square; calling an oval a circle; and accepting a shape with a gap or a curved side as a triangle. These slips come from only seeing textbook pictures. Show shapes turned, stretched and in real things.","In the lab your child taps each corner of a triangle, a square and a rectangle to count it, and finds that a circle has none. Then they sort triangles from look-alikes, and squares from rectangles, using marks that show equal sides. They build each shape from short and long sticks, and hunt for the four shapes in a room."]},"familyTip":"Go on a shape walk at home or outside. Point and ask: is that a circle, a square, a triangle or a rectangle? How many corners can you count? Cut a sandwich into triangles, then into squares.","keyIdea":"A triangle has 3 straight sides and 3 corners. A square has 4 straight sides that are all the same length and 4 corners. A rectangle has 4 sides too, 2 long and 2 short. A circle is round all the way, with no straight sides and no corners. Turning a shape round or making it bigger does not change its name, but a gap or a bendy side does. Shapes are all around: a plate is a circle, a window can be a square, a door is a rectangle and half a sandwich can be a triangle.","visual":{"type":"sorter","caption":"How many straight sides? Drag each card, or tap it and then tap a box.","opts":{"bins":["▲ 3 sides","■ 4 sides","● No sides, round"],"items":[["half a sandwich",0],["a plate",2],["a door",1],["a window",1],["a warning sign",0],["a coin",2],["a book",1],["a wheel",2]]}},"lab":{"type":"flagshape","caption":"Flagship lab: tap corners to count them, sort real triangles and squares from look-alikes, make shapes from sticks, and hunt for shapes in a room.","opts":{"step":0}}},"mt_oDlduFnemk":{"mastery":{"passMark":3,"draw":4,"pool":[{"type":"mc","id":"m1","prompt":"(5, 0) lies…","choices":["on the x-axis","on the y-axis","at the origin"],"answer":0,"hint":"y = 0 means no distance up.","explain":"It is 5 across and 0 up, so it is on the x-axis."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m2","prompt":"How far is it from (2, {a}) to (2, {b})?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Same x, so subtract the y-coordinates.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} units.","params":{"a":[0,4],"b":[5,10]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m3","prompt":"In (3, 7), which number tells you how far up?","choices":["7","3","10"],"answer":0,"hint":"The second number.","explain":"y comes second: 7 up."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m4","prompt":"A square has corners (0, 0), ({s}, 0), ({s}, {s}) and (0, {s}). What is its perimeter?","answer":"4*s","hint":"Each side is {s} long.","explain":"4 × {s} = {=4*s} units.","params":{"s":[1,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m5","prompt":"Are (2, 6) and (6, 2) the same point?","choices":["No: the order matters","Yes","Only on a big grid"],"answer":0,"hint":"Across first, then up.","explain":"(2, 6) is 2 across; (6, 2) is 6 across. They are different."},{"type":"numeric","id":"m6","prompt":"A rectangle has corners (1, 1), ({a}, 1), ({a}, {b}) and (1, {b}). What is its area?","answer":"(a-1)*(b-1)","hint":"Width = {a} − 1, height = {b} − 1.","explain":"{=a-1} × {=b-1} = {=(a-1)*(b-1)} square units.","params":{"a":[3,9],"b":[3,9]}},{"type":"mc","id":"m7","prompt":"To plot (4, 1), you go…","choices":["4 across, then 1 up","4 up, then 1 across","1 across, then 4 up"],"answer":0,"hint":"Along the corridor, then up the stairs.","explain":"4 across, then 1 up."}]},"practice":[{"type":"mc","id":"p1","prompt":"Which point is 4 across and 2 up?","choices":["(4, 2)","(2, 4)","(4, 4)"],"answer":0,"hint":"Across first.","explain":"Across first, then up: (4, 2)."},{"type":"numeric","id":"p2","prompt":"How far is it from ({a}, 3) to ({b}, 3)?","answer":"b-a","hint":"Same height, so subtract the x-coordinates.","explain":"{b} − {a} = {=b-a} units.","params":{"a":[0,4],"b":[5,10]}},{"type":"numeric","id":"p3","prompt":"A point is {a} across and {b} up. What is its x-coordinate?","answer":"a","hint":"x is the across number.","explain":"The x-coordinate is {a}.","params":{"a":[1,9],"b":[1,9]},"where":"a!=b"},{"type":"mc","id":"p4","prompt":"Where is (0, 0)?","choices":["The origin, where the axes meet","The top right corner","Anywhere on the x-axis"],"answer":0,"hint":"Zero across, zero up.","explain":"(0, 0) is the origin."}],"explain":{"kid":["A grid has two number lines. The x-axis goes across. The y-axis goes up. They meet at the origin, which is (0, 0).","A point is written (x, y). Go across first, then up. Think: along the corridor, then up the stairs. (3, 5) means 3 across and 5 up."],"adult":["The ordered pair (x, y) gives the distance across, then the distance up, from the origin. Order matters: (2, 5) and (5, 2) are different points.","Points on the x-axis have y = 0; points on the y-axis have x = 0. Distances along a grid line are easy: from (1, 4) to (6, 4) is 6 − 1 = 5 units."]},"familyTip":"Play battleships on squared paper. Call out coordinates as (across, up).","keyIdea":"(x, y) means go x across along the x-axis, then y up, starting from the origin (0, 0).","visual":{"type":"coords","caption":"Move the point to (3, 5). Drag it, or use the arrow keys: across first, then up.","opts":{"max":8,"x":0,"y":0,"target":[3,5]}},"lab":{"type":"geolab","caption":"Drag each corner onto a grid point. Read its coordinates (across, then up), then take the area challenges.","opts":{"pts":[[1,1],[5,1],[5,4]],"grid":1,"area":6}}}});
